<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thutmose III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1479–1425 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thutmose III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> and one of the most successful military rulers in its history. After the co-regency with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, he conducted numerous campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, including the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a>, and consolidated Egyptian control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Syria-Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Accounts of the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> treat him as a central figure in the empire's foundation, economy and military.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii.jpg" alt="Thutmose III" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ولاء · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_Museum,_Egypt_49_(trim).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thutmose III was the son of Thutmose II by a secondary wife, Iset. His father's Great Royal Wife was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>. When his father died he was too young to rule, and estimates of his age range from two to 13 years. Hatshepsut became his regent and eventually declared herself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, though she never denied kingship to Thutmose III. For the first 22 years of his reign he was therefore a junior co-regent while she held the senior place. When he was old enough and had shown his capacity, Hatshepsut appointed him to head her armies, and he became sole ruler after her death around 1458 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The dating of the reign depends on the chronology used. The Low Chronology gives 1479 to 1425 BCE, the convention in academic circles since the 1960s, while some scholars prefer 1504 to 1450 BCE. The length of the reign is known to the day from the tomb of the commander Amenemheb, who records his death in the 54th regnal year, corresponding to 11 March 1425 BCE.</p><p>Thutmose III conducted at least 16 campaigns in 20 years, recorded in the Annals inscribed on the walls of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>. The first led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Battle of Megiddo</a>. In his eighth campaign, in Year 33, he carried boats overland, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, setting up a stele beside the one his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> had raised. To prevent rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he took hostages from the noble families of its cities. His last campaign, in Year 50, reached the fourth cataract of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. The Egyptologist James Breasted called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>He built more than 50 temples and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> more attention than any other site, adding Pylon VI, a wall around the central sanctuary and a jubilee hall in an early basilica style. Some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s monuments were defaced after her death. Earlier scholars read this as vengeance and an erasure of her memory by Thutmose III, an interpretation presented as a theory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Two years before his death, after the death of his firstborn son Amenemhat, Thutmose III appointed Amenhotep II as junior co-regent and successor. His tomb, KV34, was found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> by Victor Loret in 1898. His mummy was discovered in the Deir el-Bahari Cache in 1881, along with those of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of the 18th and 19th Dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thutmose III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Late Bronze Age, after <b>Thutmose III</b>’s campaigns brought the Levantine coast under Egyptian control, Byblos served as a loyal base for the pharaohs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hatshepsut, who ruled as pharaoh alongside the young <b>Thutmose III</b> from approximately 1479 to 1458 BCE, consolidated this inheritance through trade rather than war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mitanni soon became Egypt's chief rival in Syria, the target of <b>Thutmose III</b>'s campaign across the Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…speak of the Keftiu, usually taken to be the Cretans, and paintings in the Theban tombs of officials of Hatshepsut and <b>Thutmose III</b> in the 15th century BCE, among them Senenmut and Rekhmire, show men with long locks of hair and patterned kilts bringing Aegean vessels as gifts.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>They were trained as scribes, wore fine white linen, and were rewarded by the king: <b>Thutmose III</b> gave his official Minmose more than 150 captive “dependents”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharaoh <b>Thutmose III</b> routed a coalition led by the king of Kadesh at Megiddo, on a date traditionally reckoned to 16 April.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
